09:57:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your breath starts out near body temperature, but how it feels depends mostly on airflow speed and mixing with room air. - **Mouth wide open (“haaa”)**: Air moves slowly and reaches your hand with less mixing. It feels warm because it’s close to your body temperature. - **Lips pursed (“whooo”)**: The small opening creates a fast jet of air. That jet: - **Mixes quickly with cooler surrounding air** - **Increases convection**, carrying heat away from your skin faster - Can increase **evaporation** of moisture on your skin, which also cools it So pursed-lip breath is not necessarily dramatically colder when it leaves your mouth; it feels colder because fast-moving, mixed air removes heat from your skin more efficiently. The same principle is why a fan makes you feel cool even when the room temperature has not changed.
