09:58:06 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right that the air reaching your hand can genuinely be at a different temperature—not just “feel” different. But the main reason is still the airflow pattern, not that your lungs produce cold air when you purse your lips. Both kinds of exhaled air leave your mouth roughly warm and humid, near body temperature. With pursed lips, though, the narrow opening makes a fast, turbulent jet. Before it reaches your hand, that jet entrains and mixes with a lot of cooler room air. So the *mixture* hitting your hand can be significantly cooler than the slow, broad exhalation from an open mouth. There are two distinct effects: 1. **Actual temperature of the air at your hand** - Pursed lips: fast jet pulls in room air and mixes with it, so it may be objectively cooler by the time it arrives. - Open mouth: slower, broader air flow remains more dominated by warm exhaled air. 2. **Cooling of your skin** - The faster jet strips away the warm layer of air next to your skin and speeds evaporation of moisture. - That makes your skin cool further, much as a fan does. The pressure-drop cooling from squeezing air through your lips exists in principle, but is tiny at normal blowing pressures—not enough to explain the dramatic difference. The dominant cause is that a narrow, fast breath reaches your hand as a cooler, room-air-mixed stream and also cools your skin more effectively.
